      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e This code assumes that all files matching a given pattern are actually to be considered as KMI symbol lists. While that is mostly true, the authoritative list is in BUILD.bazel (kmi_symbol_list + additional_kmi_symbol_lists).\n\nYes, true. But that\u0027s the assumptions everywhere since symbol lists have been introduced, isn\u0027t it? `build.sh` was using it; `kleaf` is also using that pattern, it served us well until we have `.stg` and others now. Even SAC doc also refers that partner symbols lists should be `abi_gki_aarch64_partner` here: https://source.android.com/docs/core/architecture/kernel/abi-monitor#updating-ack-abi-files\n\n\u003e The union of all symbol lists is passed as an argument to CONFIG_UNUSED_KSYMS_WHITELIST and should be accessible here.\n\nThis was using the raw list before; then something broke; and I tried `CONFIG_UNUSED_KSYMS_WHITELIST` at the time but it\u0027s not set during the `vmlinux` build but only during the `modules` build (based on prints from the Makefile) if I remember correctly. It was causing headers always empty i.e. there is no runtime symbol protection everything is allowed.\n\nIf `CONFIG_UNUSED_KSYMS_WHITELIST` is always guaranteed to point the correct list; and empty value always guaranteed to point that no runtime symbol protection is needed; then we can change this implementation to use `CONFIG_UNUSED_KSYMS_WHITELIST` as make target dependency directly. Config item needs to point to the list in all cases trim/no trim/strict/ no strict/ all cases as long as there is a single symbol list for an arch to make sure headers are non empty and runtime protection is enforced.\n\nI am fine dropping this until `kleaf` is changed to make `CONFIG_UNUSED_KSYMS_WHITELIST` available or forward fix. Let me know.",
      "parentUuid": "7e3c5052_71987420",
